#%PAM-1.0
#
# PAM stack the *session leader* opens the session through,
# installed by packaging/install.sh as /etc/pam.d/inox-auth-session.
#
# Who loads it: `inox-auth --session-leader --user <name> --vt <n>`, one process per
# login, started as root by the boundary. This is the process that calls
# pam_open_session and then execs the session program as the user, so this is the
# process pam_systemd writes XDG_SESSION_ID / XDG_RUNTIME_DIR into and the process
# whose cgroup and VT become the user's logind session.
#
# Why this is a SECOND file and must never be merged into /etc/pam.d/inox-auth:
# the leader holds no credential. It has no password to check and never receives
# one (the boundary cleared it when Authenticate ended). The safe `pam` wrapper only
# opens a session on a client that has authenticated, so the leader has to run an
# auth transaction that succeeds unconditionally — that is what the `auth` line
# below is for. Putting a permit line into the authentication stack would mean
# anything that reached `pam_authenticate` on it would be accepted: a passwordless
# login for every account. The password is checked exactly once, in
# /etc/pam.d/inox-auth; this file exists so that checking it and opening a session
# for it can happen in two different processes without moving the permit anywhere
# near the password.
#
# Why `required` and not `optional`: pam_permit cannot fail, so the two are equal
# while the module is present. They differ when it is not — `optional` turns a
# missing or unreadable pam_permit.so into a line PAM silently ignores, and the
# failure then surfaces much later as a session that does not start, with a message
# that points at logind instead of at packaging. `required` fails the transaction
# where the problem is. (crates/inox-auth/src/pam.rs carries the same requirement in
# its own words.)
#
# Why `account` and `session` still walk system-login: they are the real login
# stack. `account` is where account expiry, pam_nologin and pam_access are applied
# for this login; `session` is what registers the logind session at all —
# system-login ends with `-session optional pam_systemd.so`, and pam_systemd reads
# the VT from the XDG_VTNR the root parent put in this process's environment. No
# pam_systemd, no logind session; no logind session, no seat for libseat and no
# compositor on the display. It also brings pam_env, pam_limits, pam_umask,
# pam_loginuid, pam_keyinit and the lastlog/motd/mail lines, all inherited from the
# distribution's own stack on purpose.
#
# What must not appear here: pam_securetty (the leader is not on a tty and this is
# not where a root login is decided), any password-checking auth line (pam_unix and
# friends would prompt for a password this process does not have and must never
# hold), and anything that would make this file able to authenticate somebody.
#
# When this file is missing, pam_start("inox-auth-session") fails and every
# StartSession answers `started:false` — the screen shows a failed session start,
# which looks like a rejected password but is packaging. The reason is logged by
# `journalctl -u inox-auth`.

auth       required   pam_permit.so
account    include    system-login
session    include    system-login
